<h2>A letter is one object</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<tr><th>Self-contained</th><td>No external fetches. Every image, font and byte of video travelled with it.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Readable</th><td>One file of HTML. Open technologies, plain markup, openable for as long as browsers exist.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Immutable</th><td>Identified by the hash of its content. A correction is a new letter, never a silent edit.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Attributable</th><td>Signed by its author. The signature covers the program, the content and every attachment.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Sealed in</th><td>Interactive, but with no exit: no network, no cookies, no way to report on its reader.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<h2>What people build from it</h2>
<p>An article you can keep. An invitation. An invoice that references the order it settles. A contract with four independent signatures, held in full by everyone who signed. A club with a roster — and a <a href="/use/forums/">forum with threads, votes and moderation that nobody hosts</a>, where two readers see the same posts in a different order because each ranks by the people <em>they</em> trust.</p>

<h2>Two models, side by side</h2>
<div class="scroll"><table>
<tr><th></th><th>Three-tier service</th><th>Souspli</th></tr>
<tr><th>System of record</th><td>The operator's database</td><td>The copy in each party's hands</td></tr>
<tr><th>What you receive</th><td>A rendering, valid for one session</td><td>A file, valid indefinitely</td></tr>
<tr><th>Who sets the rules</th><td>The application tier, changeable at will</td><td>The terms written into the document</td></tr>
<tr><th>Same content for everyone</th><td>No, and not verifiably so</td><td>Yes, by content hash</td></tr>
<tr><th>Archiving</th><td>Screenshots and scrapers</td><td>Copy the file</td></tr>
<tr><th>Leakage from the client</th><td>Dozens of third-party connections</td><td>None; the container has no exit</td></tr>
</table></div>

